
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Then here's what I'd suggest: [1mdecompose into three sub-projects and brainstorm the first one properly.[0m Notifications
  is the last piece, not the first.

  [1mThe pieces[0m

  1. [1mTasks core[0m — the task model, where it's stored, and the UI to create/edit/complete. Nothing above this can be
     designed without it, because "a task changed" needs a task with fields that can change.
  2. [1mChange events + subscriptions[0m — an append-only record of [3mwhat changed, on which task, by whom, when[0m, plus how a
     user expresses "I care about this one" (explicit follow, or implied by being the assignee/creator/commenter). These
     two ship together because a subscription is meaningless without an event stream to filter, and vice versa.
  3. [1mNotification delivery[0m — turning matched events into something a person sees: an in-app inbox with read/unread, and
     possibly email/push. This is the part you actually asked for, and it's almost entirely downstream plumbing once 1
     and 2 exist.

  [1mWhy this order:[0m 3 is cheap and obvious once 2 exists; 2 is impossible without 1. The one thing I'd carry backward is
  the event seam — when we design the task model in sub-project 1, we design mutations so they're [3mrecordable[0m (every
  change goes through one write path) rather than scattering direct field assignments we'd have to retrofit later.
  That's a design constraint on sub-project 1, not extra code.

  Each piece gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle. We'd brainstorm [1mTasks core[0m now and keep the notifications
  requirement in view as the thing it has to support.

  Before I ask about the task model, one question decides the shape of all three:

  [1mThe fork:[0m does state live in the browser, or on a server? This is the least reversible decision in the whole project —
  it determines whether there are accounts, whether "notify" can ever mean anything while the tab is closed, and
  whether sub-projects 2 and 3 are a filter over local data or a real backend service.

  - [1mServer-backed, multi-user[0m — accounts, shared tasks, durable event log, notifications that arrive when you're not
    looking. Largest build; needs a backend stack, a database, and auth before a single notification fires.
  - [1mBrowser-local, single-user[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, no accounts, no backend. "Notifications" degrades to in-app reminders and
    a what-changed-since-last-visit digest. Buildable in days. Retrofitting multi-user later means rewriting the
    storage layer.
